A||N||5||Lance||Just got my first Fiero||||10||
Z||000000||Lance||02-10-2000||08:26 AM||||I just got my very first Fiero on the 9th. I'ts an 85 Sports Coupe. 4 cilinder. I know its not much, nothing compered to all your suped up Fieros but its a start. I was woundering if anyone had an idea of some good upgrades. Also, does anyone know of a Fiero Club in the Kansas City Area? Thanx.||||reg||10
Z||000001||william_thorniii||02-10-2000||05:28 PM||||Lance,<P>Congratulations!  My first Fiero was a 1987 CP.  This car is bare bones.  It doesn't even have a sun roof!  I must admit though I prefer it to my bells & whistles 1985 GT.  Welcome to Fiero-ownership.  You will quickly realize that it is more like a disease or addiction than a transportation relationship.  <P>Now to address your questions, I know that dogtired is from the KS area.  I don't know much else out there although there seem to be a lot of folks from SD on here too.  To find other local owners head to the Michigan Fiero Club web page.  They have a section called F.O.L.D.  It is a database of Fiero owners.  I imagine that you will be able to find many folks there.  <P>About the modifications... there are guys and gals on this forum and elsewhere that have modified Fiero's so much that they share more with other cars than a Fiero.  Just remember be creative.  The Fiero is an excellent platform to work from.  <P>Welcome,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000002||deceler8||02-10-2000||05:45 PM||||Hey, nothing wrong with Coupes (I have an '86 2m4)...simple to work on and look good too.<P>Also tend not to have many of the expensive maintenance issues of the more souped-up brethren.<P>As to clubs, check out this link...Midwest Fieros...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Factory/4567/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Factory/4567/</A> ||||reg||10
Z||000003||ChadMan||02-11-2000||09:09 AM||||Hey, congrats, Lance!  Coupes aren't fast, but they're reliable, cheap, and lots of fun.  <P>Sadly, there isn't much you can do to hop up the Iron Duke unless you want to spend the money and time on a complete rebuild.  But you can make sure it produces the whole 92 hp that God and Pontiac intended!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Sage||02-11-2000||09:19 AM||||Lance: Congratulations and welcome to Fieroland.  An 84SE was our first and I think it is still my favorite to drive. I have never seen much point in trying to hot rod the 4 banger, but have rebuilt ours to like new condition.  Check out the Mich. site that WTIII mentioned, and any others that are passed along.  Good tires and refurbished suspension with a 4whl alignment by somebody who knows what they are doing will go a long way to providing a long lasting and enjoyable relationship with your Fiero.  Have fun and let us know how you like your new ride!||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||02-11-2000||06:00 PM||||Hey Lance, I work in Topeka, so we're not too far from each other. Dogtired and PHerder live in the Wichita area. No Kansas Fiero clubs, but maybe Missouri has one. Maybe a few of us local flatlanders ought to have an informal Fiero get together this spring. ||||reg||1
